{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And the earth was corrupted, etc.</b> The entire verse had no need to be said, for He already wrote in the preceding section (Genesis 6:5): ״וַיַּרְא ה׳ כִּי רַבָּה״, etc. And if the intention was to specify the sin, it is more explicit in the preceding section, where it is written: ״רַבָּה רָעַת הָאָדָם וְכָל יֵצֶר״, etc. And if because He did not mention there the matter of חָמָס (violence), He should have mentioned it there. Further, why in the preceding section did He say the name of mercy and here He said the name Elohim? Further, why was it necessary to say the word ״לִפְנֵי הָאֱלֹהִים״ (before the Elohim)? Further, why did He return again to say <b>וַיַּרְא אֱלֹהִים</b>, etc.? If to inform that HaShem knows everything that happens — this is already known." "Now the matter will be explained according to their saying in Avot (Avot 4:11), and this is their language: one who commits a single transgression acquires for himself a kategor (prosecutor) — meaning, a destroying angel. But you must know that the destroyer cannot do anything before the Judge of all the earth renders judgment, for HaShem restrains them from doing evil, and this is the sod of the interpretation of (Exodus 34:7) ״נוֹשֵׂא עָוֹן״. And after Elohim renders judgment, He then delivers them into the hand of their transgression — they being the kategorim that are created through their evil deeds, as it says (Jeremiah 2:19): ״תְּיַסְּרֵךְ רָעָתֵךְ״. And you should know that although HaShem restrains the prosecutor from harming its author, nevertheless when they multiply — it will press a complaint that can no longer be endured, and it will press to hasten its action to destroy its owners before their trial is concluded, for its judgment is explicit — and that is His saying <b>and the earth was corrupted before the Elohim</b> — meaning, before their judgment was concluded the earth was corrupted, that is, destroying forces were installed in it on account of their deeds. And He was precise in saying <b>the earth</b> — the intention being that even the body of the earth, on account of the severity of the sins, their portion in the earth was cursed, for the foulness arising from their deeds was lodged within it. And His saying <b>and the earth was filled</b> — logically the cause should have been stated first and then the effect, which is the creation of the destroyer; rather, the intention was to inform that after they reached this threshold they continued to make things fouler than before." "<b>חָמָס</b> — meaning: חָמָס is the totality of wickedness — it includes robbery, it includes illicit relations, it includes bloodshed, it includes idolatry, as our Sages of blessed memory demonstrated (Genesis Rabbah 31) from the verses — see their words there. And although His attribute, blessed be He, is to bear iniquity until the time the sentence arrives, that generation's sin was too great to be borne under the attribute of bearing iniquity, in the manner of Cain's statement (Genesis 4:13): ״גָּדוֹל עֲוֹנִי מִנְּשׂוֹא״. Or it may mean <b>and the earth was corrupted</b> — these are their evil deeds, and on this account the earth was filled with prosecutors designated by the name חָמָס, and this is what the verse informed Noah in saying ״קֵץ כָּל בָּשָׂר בָּא לְפָנַי״ — meaning: those prosecutors that were created from the inhabitants of the earth, who are called קֵץ, as is explicit in the words of the Zohar (part 1, p. 63), have come before Me and press a complaint to hasten their destruction. And the reason for informing all these verses is to say that without this HaShem would have prolonged His anger for some time, and although He said in the preceding section (Genesis 6:7) ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ אֶמְחֶה אֶת הָאָדָם״, etc., nevertheless HaShem would relent concerning the evil and prolong His anger; but on account of the severity of the destroyer — and that is His saying <b>וַיַּרְא אֱלֹהִים</b>, etc., and behold it was corrupted with severity — for this reason HaShem hastened to say to Noah ״עֲשֵׂה לְךָ״, etc. And the reason He mentioned the name Elohim in this section is to show that even the attribute of mercy was transformed into the attribute of justice on account of the deed of the wicked." "<b>The earth — חָמָס.</b> In the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 31) and this is their language: he would bring a basket of lupines, and each person would take one lupine and could not bring a claim in court — end quote. The early commentators already preceded and raised the difficulty that the children of Noah were killed for less than the value of a perutah. And it appears to me that the Midrash reveals that besides nullifying the core principle of the Torah's seven commandments, they further committed violence against the law of human reason, for robbing a perutah is something whose return one could demand, being no less than the value of a perutah, and they would devise schemes to rob in a manner that no claim could be brought against them. Also according to the law it appears to me that the Torah did not obligate the children of Noah except for money that comes into one's hands by way of robbery; but in the case of credit extension one would not be liable. And although the Rambam in chapter 9 of Hilkhot Melakhim wrote that withholdders of a hired worker's wages are also killed for it under the laws of Noah, that is not comparable to this case which comes to hand by way of a loan, where the borrower says ״I am obligated to pay you when the time comes״, and those people would take voluntarily on credit, to repay afterward, and when the owner was willing to extend credit they would take less than the value of a perutah, and in the laws of loans they would not be judged for less than the value of a perutah if he is liable to him when he claims ״I repaid״ or ״you have collateral against it״, and this matter is not in the category of the commandment of robbery that would incur the death penalty, where one would say ״I thought you had nothing against me at all״. Further it is possible to say that they could claim under the law of gift that what comes to hand was given to acquire from him by way of purchase."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {3 "The word הַקָּפָה appears repeatedly here in a commercial-legal sense; I render it as 'credit extension' based on context (the discussion of loans and repayment), though the precise halakhic-technical usage is unusual and the reading is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:40:02.067937185Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}